# C#/.NET coding style

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic style rules to modern C#
idiom, following the .NET Framework Design Guidelines and C# coding
conventions: nullable reference types (on by default in new templates since
.NET 6/C# 10; an existing project needs `<Nullable>enable</Nullable>`
added), primary constructors and collection expressions (C# 12/.NET 8).
Applies only to `*.cs` files.

## Naming and structure

- A private field is `_camelCase`, not `m_field` or bare `camelCase`.
- Interfaces are prefixed `I` (`IOrderRepository`), never suffixed.
- One public type per file, named after the type, unless the type is a small
  private nested/support type.
- File-scoped namespaces (`namespace Orders;`) unless the project's existing
  files use block-scoped ones.

## Records, classes, and immutability

- `record`/`record class` for a type whose identity is its value (DTO,
  command, event, immutable snapshot); `class` for identity beyond data,
  mutable lifetime state, or ORM-tracked entity semantics.
- `record struct` for a small, frequently-allocated value type where heap
  allocation matters; reference `record` otherwise.
- `init`-only properties (or a primary constructor) over a public setter on
  anything that should not change after construction.

## Pattern matching and modern idiom

- Pattern matching (`is`, `switch` expressions, property and list patterns)
  over `if`/`else if` type-check chains or cast-then-check.
- A primary constructor (`class OrderService(IOrderRepository repo)`) for a
  type whose constructor only assigns parameters to fields; an explicit
  constructor once it needs validation or logic.
- Collection expressions (`int[] xs = [1, 2, 3];`, `List<T> ys = [];`) in
  new code unless the project has not adopted them; do not mix both styles
  in one file.
- Expression-bodied members for single-expression members; a block body once
  more than one statement is needed, no `;` chaining or nested ternaries.

## Nullable reference types

- Keep `<Nullable>enable</Nullable>` on; no `<Nullable>disable</Nullable>` or
  blanket `#nullable disable` to silence warnings.
- A parameter, property, or return type that can be absent is annotated `?`;
  a member without `?` never returns null.
- `ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(value)` at a public API's entry point,
  not a hand-rolled `if (value is null) throw ...`.
- Resolve a nullability warning by fixing the null path; the null-forgiving
  operator (`value!`) only at a boundary with a comment stating why the value
  is provably non-null.

## `var` usage

- `var` when the right-hand side makes the type obvious
  (`var order = new Order();`) or the type is long/generic and adds no
  clarity.
- An explicit type when `var` would hide a non-obvious return type
  (`var result = Parse(input);`) or when the declared type differs from the
  initializer's runtime type.
